# Reflections

I learned that information dashboards are not just about packing many charts onto one page, but about choosing visual idioms - such as bullet charts and sparklines - that let a viewer judge performance against a target or trend at a glance. Bullet charts were a useful reminder that a single bar can encode several reference bands (poor, satisfactory, good) plus a target marker, all without needing a separate legend. Sparklines showed me how `gt`/`gtExtras` and `reactable`/`reactablefmtr` take the same idea further by embedding a miniature trend line directly inside a table cell, which keeps the table compact while still conveying the shape of the data over time. Comparing the static (`gt`/`gtExtras`) and interactive (`reactable`/`reactablefmtr`) approaches also helped me see the trade-off between a dashboard meant for a printed/exported report versus one meant to be explored on screen.

# Introduction

By the end of this hands-on exercise, we will be able to:

-   Create a bullet chart using **ggplot2**.

-   Create sparklines using **ggplot2**.

-   Build a static information dashboard using **gt** and **gtExtras**.

-   Build an interactive information dashboard using **reactable** and **reactablefmtr**.

# Getting Started

The following R packages will be used for this hands-on exercise.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
pacman::p_load(lubridate, ggthemes, reactable,
               reactablefmtr, gt, gtExtras, tidyverse)
```
:::


-   **tidyverse** provides a collection of packages for importing, tidying, wrangling and visualising data, including **readr**, **tidyr**, **dplyr**, **ggplot2**, **tibble**, **stringr**, **forcats** and **purrr**.

-   **lubridate** provides functions to work with dates and times more efficiently.

-   **ggthemes** is an extension of **ggplot2** that provides additional themes beyond the basic ones.

-   **gtExtras** provides helper functions to assist in creating tables with **gt**, a package for making polished tables in R.

-   **reactable** provides functions to create interactive data tables for R, based on the React Table library.

-   **reactablefmtr** streamlines and enhances the styling of interactive **reactable** tables with customisable functions and themes.

# Importing Microsoft Access Database

## The data set

For this exercise, a personal database in Microsoft Access mdb format called *Coffee Chain* is used.

## Importing database into R

`odbcConnectAccess()` of **RODBC** package is used to import a database query table into R.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(RODBC)
con <- odbcConnectAccess2007('data/Coffee Chain.mdb')
coffeechain <- sqlFetch(con, 'CoffeeChain Query')
write_rds(coffeechain, "data/CoffeeChain.rds")
odbcClose(con)
```
:::


**Note:** before running the code chunk above, the R system needs to be switched to the 32-bit version, because `odbcConnectAccess()` is based on 32-bit, not 64-bit. As the rds file has already been produced, this chunk is not re-run here.

## Data Preparation

The code chunk below imports `CoffeeChain.rds` into R.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
coffeechain <- read_rds("data/CoffeeChain.rds")
```
:::


The code chunk below aggregates Sales and Budgeted Sales at the Product level.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
product <- coffeechain %>%
  group_by(`Product`) %>%
  summarise(`target` = sum(`Budget Sales`),
            `current` = sum(`Sales`)) %>%
  ungroup()
```
:::


## Bullet chart in ggplot2

The code chunk below plots a bullet chart using **ggplot2** functions.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(product, aes(Product, current)) +
  geom_col(aes(Product, max(target) * 1.01),
           fill = "grey85", width = 0.85) +
  geom_col(aes(Product, target * 0.75),
           fill = "grey60", width = 0.85) +
  geom_col(aes(Product, target * 0.5),
           fill = "grey50", width = 0.85) +
  geom_col(aes(Product, current),
           width = 0.35,
           fill = "black") +
  geom_errorbar(aes(y = target,
                    x = Product,
                    ymin = target,
                    ymax = target),
                width = .4,
                colour = "red",
                linewidth = 1) +
  coord_flip()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](Hands-on_Ex10_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-5-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


# Plotting Sparklines Using ggplot2

## Preparing the data


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
sales_report <- coffeechain %>%
  filter(Date >= "2013-01-01") %>%
  mutate(Month = month(Date)) %>%
  group_by(Month, Product) %>%
  summarise(Sales = sum(Sales)) %>%
  ungroup() %>%
  select(Month, Product, Sales)
```
:::


The code chunk below computes the minimum, maximum and end-of-month sales.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
mins <- group_by(sales_report, Product) %>%
  slice(which.min(Sales))
maxs <- group_by(sales_report, Product) %>%
  slice(which.max(Sales))
ends <- group_by(sales_report, Product) %>%
  filter(Month == max(Month))
```
:::


The code chunk below computes the 25th and 75th quantiles.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
quarts <- sales_report %>%
  group_by(Product) %>%
  summarise(quart1 = quantile(Sales, 0.25),
            quart2 = quantile(Sales, 0.75)) %>%
  right_join(sales_report)
```
:::


## Sparklines in ggplot2


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(sales_report, aes(x = Month, y = Sales)) +
  facet_grid(Product ~ ., scales = "free_y") +
  geom_ribbon(data = quarts, aes(ymin = quart1, ymax = quart2),
              fill = 'grey90') +
  geom_line(linewidth = 0.3) +
  geom_point(data = mins, col = 'red') +
  geom_point(data = maxs, col = 'blue') +
  geom_text(data = mins, aes(label = Sales), vjust = -1) +
  geom_text(data = maxs, aes(label = Sales), vjust = 2.5) +
  geom_text(data = ends, aes(label = Sales), hjust = 0, nudge_x = 0.5) +
  geom_text(data = ends, aes(label = Product), hjust = 0, nudge_x = 1.0) +
  expand_limits(x = max(sales_report$Month) +
                  (0.25 * (max(sales_report$Month) - min(sales_report$Month)))) +
  scale_x_continuous(breaks = seq(1, 12, 1)) +
  scale_y_continuous(expand = c(0.1, 0)) +
  theme_tufte(base_size = 7, base_family = "Helvetica") +
  theme(axis.title = element_blank(), axis.text.y = element_blank(),
        axis.ticks = element_blank(), strip.text = element_blank())
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](Hands-on_Ex10_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-9-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


# Static Information Dashboard Design: gt and gtExtras Methods

In this section, a static information dashboard is created using **gt** and **gtExtras** packages.

## Plotting a simple bullet chart


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
product %>%
  gt::gt() %>%
  gt_plt_bullet(column = current,
                target = target,
                width = 60,
                palette = c("lightblue",
                            "black")) %>%
  gt_theme_538()
```

